                "text": "<p>Every cook knows the moment. Guests are hungry, the roast looks done, and you're stabbing it with a thermometer for the fourth time, watching the juices run out of every hole. A single reading tells you where the meat is right now. It can't tell you when dinner will be ready, and if you hit the wrong spot it can't even tell you that.</p><p>The Combustion Predictive Thermometer was built to answer the question cooks actually ask: <strong>how long until it's done?</strong> It was co-designed by Chris Young, a former Modernist Cuisine chef, and J. Kenji López-Alt calls it game-changing. Here are six reasons it's replacing the old probe in kitchens and on grills.</p>",
